Generation investment now reaches $1 trillion a year, yet the federal budget still leaves grid connections unspecified. This is environmental intelligence, episode 76. Carbon pricing politics, the assessment flags that ride with the new grid money, and the week ahead. The federal budget, the Globe and Mail reports, puts money on expanding power grids so renewable generation can be tied in. Since 2015, spending on electricity generation, the kind that builds new power plants,

00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:36.220
has climbed nearly 70%. Over that same stretch, spending on the wires rose at less than half

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:41.920
the pace. Earlier federal approaches put the weight on generation. The new allocations aim

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.180
at upgrades. The international energy agency calculates that yearly investment in grids

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:53.480
has to rise about 50% from the $400 billion a year going in now. Leave that shortfall in

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:59.000
place, the agency warns, and global emissions climb because fossil fuel plants stay online

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.540
longer. Jan Rosenau, a professor of energy and climate policy at Oxford University,

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.700
says the grids were never prepared for renewable generation at this scale. He calls the

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:15.100
past complacent, and says the grid has become a real bottleneck. What I find notable is

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.260
a domestic budget line landing on a bottleneck the agency is already pricing in global emissions.

